[dvystrcil/homelab] Track Longhorn disk usage per node over time with alerts  (model=qwen3-coder-next:latest, 66s, lane=claude)
## Why
Longhorn is now the backing storage for every Postgres cluster. As disk consumption grows, there's no per-node historical tracking or proactive alerting before a disk fills up. This creates risk of unavailability when a node's Longhorn data path reaches capacity.

## What we know
- Longhorn exposes filesystem metrics via its metrics endpoint (longhorn/values.yaml).
- The homelab already scrapes Prometheus metrics and has alerting rules (prometheus repo, homelab architecture docs).
- Issue https://github.com/dvystrcil/homelab/issues/1982 explicitly tracks Longhorn disk consumption growth and notes 83 orphaned objects totaling 128.6 GiB on hpm-01 by 2026-11-07 (longhorn/.github/workflows/helm-validate.yaml).
- Node-level disk usage is critical because Longhorn distributes replicas across nodes and each node must have sufficient free space for its assigned replicas.

## Acceptance criteria
- [ ] **AC1.** A new dashboard panel in Grafana shows per-node Longhorn disk usage (used/total) over the last 7 days.
- [ ] **AC2.** A new Prometheus alert rule fires at 80% and 95% per-node disk utilization, sending to the ops channel.
- [ ] **AC3.** The alert includes the node name and volume name(s) consuming the most space.

## Lane
claude — requires design decisions: which metrics to surface, how to aggregate per-node usage from Longhorn's metrics, and where to place the alert logic (Prometheus vs. Longhorn UI). Also touches networking/storage and may require changes to the Prometheus scrape config for Longhorn metrics.

